SUMMARY:New Year's Day
DESCRIPTION:History: The Roman calendar fixed the turn of the civil year to
  January 1 under Julius Caesar\, and the Gregorian reform of 1582 reaffirm
 ed the date for much of the world.\nTraditions: Communities ring bells\, c
 ount down in packed squares\, exchange resolutions\, and watch fireworks o
 r televised celebrations to welcome a clean slate.\nFeasting: Families toa
 st with sparkling wine\, share longevity noodles or Hoppin' John for luck\
 , and slice sweet breads like panettone or king cakes to invite prosperity
 .\nDay: Thursday\n\nIcon: 🎊\nCategory: celebration
CATEGORIES:celebration
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/new-years-day-champagne-and-confetti.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260102T070000Z
DTEND:20260103T070000Z
SUMMARY:Perchta Day (Perchtenlauf)
DESCRIPTION:History: Alpine folklore honors Frau Perchta\, a wintry guardia
 n of hearth and spinning who inspected homes during the Twelve Nights afte
 r Christmas.\nTraditions: Villages in Austria and Bavaria host Perchtenlae
 ufe where masked figures parade with cowbells and torches to chase away da
 rkness and protect the fields.\nFeasting: Hosts offer dumplings\, poppy-se
 ed pastries\, and milky punches to the performers\, along with mulled wine
  and sweet krapfen for the crowd.\nDay: Friday\n\nIcon: 🎭\nCategory: cu
 ltural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/austrian-and-bavarian-tradition-perchta-day.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260102T070000Z
DTEND:20260103T070000Z
SUMMARY:Berchtoldstag
DESCRIPTION:History: Medieval Swiss guilds observed Berchtoldstag as a post
 -feast day to honor community bonds and start the civic year together.\nTra
 ditions: Families make Berchteln visits\, children knock on doors during Ba
 erzelistag parades\, and neighbors share well wishes with carved masks or p
 ine boughs.\nFeasting: Tables feature braided Zopf loaves\, roesti skillets
 \, sausages\, hazelnut tortes\, and pots of melted cheese for communal dipp
 ing.\nDay: Friday\n\nIcon: 🍞\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260102T070000Z
DTEND:20260103T070000Z
SUMMARY:Candlemas (Feast of the Presentation)
DESCRIPTION:History: Commemorated in Jerusalem by the fourth century\, Cand
 lemas marks forty days after Christmas when Mary and Joseph presented Jesu
 s at the Temple according to Mosaic law.\nTraditions: Churches bless candl
 es for the coming year\, processions illuminate winter nights\, and weathe
 r lore predicts the remaining length of winter.\nFeasting: La Chandeleur t
 ables offer crepes beurre sucre\, crepes jambon-fromage\, boles de cidre b
 rut\, and salade de mache\; in Mexico\, familias sirven tamales oaxaquenos
 \, tamales de dulce\, atole de canela\, champurrado espumoso\, and pan de 
 candelaria after the blessing of candles.\nDay: Friday\n\nIcon: 🕯️\nC
 ategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/candlemas-feast-of-the-presentation-of-jesus-and-blessing-of-candles.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260105T070000Z
DTEND:20260106T070000Z
SUMMARY:Twelfth Night
DESCRIPTION:History: Twelfth Night concludes the Christmastide counting fro
 m medieval Europe\, blending Christian Epiphany vigils with older winter r
 evels.\nTraditions: Hosts crown a king or queen with hidden bean charms\, 
 wassailers sing at doorways\, and revelers dismantle greenery to avoid a y
 ear of bad fortune.\nFeasting: Tables feature spiced ale\, figgy puddings\
 , king cakes filled with almonds\, and roasted meats shared before the fas
 ting of Epiphany begins.\nDay: Monday\n\nIcon: 🎭\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/twelfth-night-end-of-the-christmas-season.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260106T070000Z
DTEND:20260107T070000Z
SUMMARY:Epiphany (Three Kings Day)
DESCRIPTION:History: Since the second century\, Epiphany has celebrated the
  Magi recognising the Christ child\, symbolising the revelation of the div
 ine to the nations.\nTraditions: Families bless doorways with chalk\, stag
 e pageants of camels and kings\, and children receive gifts left by los Re
 yes Magos or La Befana.\nFeasting: Bakers craft rosca de reyes\, galette d
 es rois\, or king cakes\, while households sip rich hot chocolate and shar
 e slow-simmered stews.\nDay: Tuesday\n\nIcon: 👑\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/epiphany-three-kings-day-celebration-of-the-magi.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260110T070000Z
DTEND:20260111T070000Z
SUMMARY:Fete de Papa Zaka - Feast of the Farmer Loa
DESCRIPTION:History: Haitian Vodou communities honour Papa Zaka, the kindly
  farmer lwa, during January gatherings to thank him for guarding crops and
  labouring families.\nTraditions: Altars are dressed with straw hats, ears
  of corn, bowls of grain, rum, and pipe tobacco while drummers, singers, a
 nd vodouisants petition Zaka for fertile fields before sharing food with n
 eighbours.\nFeasting: Aperitifs pour clairin ti punch, kleren anisette, an
 d pumpkin-spice jus giraumon; first courses carry soup joumou, accra de mo
 rue with pikliz, and mayi moulen ak sos pwa; mains feature poulet nan sos,
  griot de porc, legim nan diri, and red beans with rice; sides bring banna
 nn peze, pain mayi, and jars of pikliz; desserts finish with pen patat, bo
 nbons sirop, tropical fruits, and altar offerings of roasted corn, a grain
 -filled straw hat, and a shot of rum with pipe tobacco.\nDay: Saturday\n\n
 Icon: 🌾\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260110T070000Z
DTEND:20260111T070000Z
SUMMARY:Fete du Vodoun (Benin Vodoun Festival)
DESCRIPTION:History: Benin recognised Vodoun as a national religion in 1996
 \, formalising annual January gatherings that honour ancestral spirits and
  the transatlantic heritage of resistance.\nTraditions: Processions of pri
 ests\, devotees\, and Egungun dancers drum along Ouidah's Route des Esclav
 es\, offering libations at the Temple of Pythons and the seaside.\nFeastin
 g: Festival goers share akassa corn porridge\, goat and fish stews flavour
 ed with palm oil\, toasted peanuts\, and generous pours of sodabi palm liq
 uor.\nDay: Saturday\n\nIcon: 🎭\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/vodoun-festival-traditional-celebration-in-benin-honoring-vodoun-spirituality-an.png
END:VEVENT
