DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260115T070000Z
DTEND:20260116T070000Z
SUMMARY:Dondoyaki Bonfire
DESCRIPTION:History: Dondoyaki descends from Heian-period shrine rites that
  respectfully retire shimenawa ropes\, daruma\, and calligraphy written du
 ring the New Year festivities.\nTraditions: Communities pile pine branches
  and lucky charms into tall pyres\, send sparks skyward with communal pray
 ers\, and children roast lucky rice cakes in the embers.\nFeasting: Skewer
 ed mochi toasted over the flames\, sweet azuki bean soup\, and cups of war
 m amazake fortify neighbours against the cold night air.\nDay: Thursday\n\
 nIcon: 🔥\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/japanese-new-year-tradition-of-burning-new-year-decorations.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260117T070000Z
DTEND:20260118T070000Z
SUMMARY:Old Twelfth Night Wassail
DESCRIPTION:History: When Britain adopted the Gregorian calendar in 1752\, 
 many West Country farmers kept the old January date for Twelfth Night to b
 less orchards against blight.\nTraditions: Wassailers bang pans\, fire mus
 kets into branches\, and sing to wake the apple trees while offering cider
 -soaked toast to the guardian robin.\nFeasting: Hot mulled cider\, apple c
 akes\, blue cheese\, and pork pies fuel the rounds from farm to farm throu
 gh the frosty fields.\nDay: Saturday\n\nIcon: 🌟\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/old-twelfth-night-traditional-date-before-calendar-reform.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260125T070000Z
DTEND:20260126T070000Z
SUMMARY:Burns Night Supper
DESCRIPTION:History: The first Burns Night was held in 1801 by friends of R
 obert Burns\, celebrating the Bard's January birth with readings that ceme
 nted his role in Scottish identity.\nTraditions: Kilted hosts pipe in the 
 haggis\, deliver the Address with flourish\, toast the lassies\, and end w
 ith ceilidh dances and "Auld Lang Syne."\nFeasting: Menus centre on haggis
  with neeps and tatties\, cock-a-leekie soup\, cranachan\, oatcakes\, and 
 drams of smoky single malt whisky.\nDay: Sunday\n\nIcon: 🏴󠁧󠁢󠁳
 󠁣󠁴󠁿\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/scottish-celebration-of-the-poet-robert-burns-with-poetry-haggis-and-whisky.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20260911T170000Z
DTSTART:20260127T070000Z
DTEND:20260128T070000Z
SUMMARY:Feast of St. Muirgen (Lí Ban the Mermaid)
DESCRIPTION:History: Lí Ban\, daughter of Eochaid\, survived the bursting 
 of Lough Neagh in a chamber beneath the waves and\, after a year\, was tra
 nsformed into a mermaid\, half woman and half salmon\, with her lapdog as 
 an otter. She swam the seas for three centuries until fishermen of St. Com
 gall of Bangor netted her at Larne Lough\; baptised Muirgen ("sea-born")\,
  she chose heaven at once and is venerated on 27 January.\nTraditions: Rea
 d her tale from the Annals of the Four Masters\, walk the shores of Lough 
 Neagh or Larne Lough\, and light a candle for sailors and all who live by 
 the sea.\nFeasting: A coastal table of poached Irish salmon\, dulse and ca
 rrageen pudding\, Bangor oysters\, brown soda bread with salted butter\, a
 nd a pot of tea or a measure of Bushmills.\nDay: Tuesday\n\nIcon: 🧜‍
 ♀️\nCategory: religious
CATEGORIES:religious
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/st-muirgen-mermaid.svg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260129T070000Z
DTEND:20260130T070000Z
SUMMARY:Lunar New Year (Chunjie)
DESCRIPTION:History: Rooted in Han dynasty agrarian cycles\, the Lunar New 
 Year welcomes spring with family reunions\, ancestor veneration\, and hope
 s for prosperity.\nTraditions: Homes are scrubbed\, red lanterns strung\, 
 hongbao envelopes gifted\, and lion or dragon dances chase away lingering 
 Nian monsters.\nFeasting: Reunion banquets showcase longevity noodles\, wh
 ole fish\, dumplings shaped like ingots\, sweet rice cakes\, and mandarin 
 oranges for abundance.\nDay: Thursday\n\nIcon: 🐉\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/lunar-new-year-celebration.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260129T070000Z
DTEND:20260130T070000Z
SUMMARY:Losar (Tibetan New Year)
DESCRIPTION:History: Losar blends ancient Bon winter rites with Buddhist ob
 servances\, dating to the era of King Trisong Detsen and now marking the T
 ibetan calendar's turning.\nTraditions: Families replace prayer flag strea
 mers\, visit monasteries for cham dances\, and exchange khata scarves whil
 e reciting auspicious wishes.\nFeasting: Households prepare guthuk noodle 
 soup with symbolic dumplings\, deep-fried khapse pastries\, dried yak chee
 se\, and butter tea shared with neighbours.\nDay: Thursday\n\nIcon: 🏔
 ️\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/tibetan-new-year-celebration-of-the-lunar-new-year-in-tibetan-culture.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260130T070000Z
DTEND:20260131T070000Z
SUMMARY:Imbolc Eve (La Fheile Bride)
DESCRIPTION:History: Imbolc heralds the first stirrings of spring in ancien
 t Gaelic tradition\, bridged later with the veneration of Saint Brigid of 
 Kildare.\nTraditions: Families fashion Brigid's crosses\, set out corn dol
 lies to receive the saint's blessing\, and keep vigil fires burning throug
 h the night.\nFeasting: Dairy-rich dishes--fresh butter\, soft cheeses\, o
 atcakes\, and early lamb stews--honour Brigid's patronage of livestock and
  the hearth.\nDay: Friday\n\nIcon: 🌙\nCategory: seasonal
CATEGORIES:seasonal
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/eve-of-imbolc-the-celtic-festival-marking-the-beginning-of-spring.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20260201T070000Z
DTEND:20260202T070000Z
SUMMARY:Imbolc Day Celebration
DESCRIPTION:History: With the lambing season underway\, Imbolc marks the mi
 dpoint between solstice and equinox\, long tied to the rebirth of light an
 d Brigid's protective cloak.\nTraditions: Pilgrims visit holy wells\, spri
 nkle thresholds with blessed water\, and hang swaddles outdoors to absorb 
 Brigid's healing power.\nFeasting: Communities share bannocks\, colcannon\
 , dulse\, and honeyed porridges\, often concluding with tea infused with t
 he first tender herbs.\nDay: Sunday\n\nIcon: 🌱\nCategory: seasonal
CATEGORIES:seasonal
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/eve-of-imbolc-the-celtic-festival-marking-the-beginning-of-spring.png
END:VEVENT
