SUMMARY:Día de Muertos / Día de los Angelitos
DESCRIPTION:History: On 1 November\, Mexican families honour children who h
 ave died\, believing their angelitos return first to enjoy the love of the
 ir kin.\nTraditions: Altars glow with candles\, toys\, and paper cut-outs\
 , while families ring bells and scatter marigolds to guide the little spir
 its home.\nFeasting: Offerings feature atole\, sugar skulls\, tiny pan de
  muerto loaves\, fruit\, and favourite sweets that delighted the children
  in life. At home\, enjoy candied pumpkin (calabaza en tacha) simmered in
  piloncillo and cinnamon syrup. At home\, share tamales with warm atole.\n
 At-home activity: Host a tamalada—a gathering of family or friends to ma
 ke tamales together. Share the work of spreading masa\, filling\, folding
  husks\, and steaming while exchanging stories. Customs vary by family and
  region.\nDay: Saturday\nFilms: At home\, watch Coco (2017) or The Book of
  Life (2014).\n2026 community events (November 1):\nChildren’s Museum of
  Denver: 9 a.m. start\; activities included with admission\, members free.
  Ofrenda\, kite making\, stories\, face painting\, tastings and mariachi p
 arade. 2121 Children’s Museum Drive\, Denver.\nFine Arts Center: 12:30
 –4:30 p.m.\; free\, RSVP encouraged. Community ofrenda\, performances\,
  art activities\, mercado and children’s parade. 30 West Dale St.\, Colo
 rado Springs. These are separate venues\; choose a visit to suit your day.
  Future venue dates require organizer confirmation.\nChildren’s Museum:
  https://www.mychildsmuseum.org/events/EventDetail/day-of-the-dead\nFine A
 rts Center: https://fac.coloradocollege.edu/events/dia-de-muertos/\n\nIcon
 : 👼\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
