DTEND:20251223T070000Z
SUMMARY:Polaris Meteor Watch & UFO Evening
DESCRIPTION:History: Stargazers gather during the solstice week to trace meteor showers as they skim past Polaris, blending folk UFO lore with real night-sky science.\nTraditions: Neighbors hang neon saucer lanterns, align telescopes north, lead children in meteor-counting logs, and play synth carols while a laser pointer traces the Little Dipper.\nFeasting: Camps pour midnight cocoa tinted nebula-blue, share smoky chili, citrus-rosemary popcorn, and shortbread saucers dusted with edible stardust.\nDay: Monday\n\nIcon: 🛸\nCategory: seasonal
CATEGORIES:seasonal
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/festive-ufo-meteor-watch.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20251220T190000Z
DTEND:20251221T070000Z
SUMMARY:Yalda Night
DESCRIPTION:History: Persian Yalda predates Islam\, celebrating the birth o
 f Mithra and later the victory of light over darkness on the longest night
 .\nTraditions: Families stay awake reading Hafez poetry\, storytelling\, a
 nd watching the fire's glow until the first light of dawn.\nFeasting: Aper
 itif glasses of sharbat-e anar o gol-e sorkh—pomegranate and rosewater c
 ordial with mint and lime—open the sofreh before meze of sabzi khordan w
 ith herbs\, feta\, walnuts\, and warm flatbread\, borani laboo beet yogurt
  with pistachio\, and zeytoon parvardeh olives in pomegranate molasses. Th
 e main spread features fesenjān of duck or chicken simmered to a glossy w
 alnut-pomegranate sauce\, tahchin-e zereshk saffron rice cake layered with
  barberries and tender chicken\, and khoresht-e bamieh okra stew for veget
 arians. Salad-e Shirazi and sesame-topped naan-e barbari brighten the tabl
 e\, while desserts showcase anar o hendevaneh fruit\, ajil-e Shab-e Yalda 
 roasted nuts and dried fruits for good fortune\, and sholeh zard saffron r
 ice pudding dusted with cinnamon. Guests close the vigil by drawing Hafez 
 divinations as poetry and the promise of returning light fill the night.\n
 Day: Saturday\n\nIcon: 🌙\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/eve-of-the-winter-solstice-the-longest-night.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20251223T070000Z
DTEND:20251224T070000Z
SUMMARY:La Noche de Rabanos
DESCRIPTION:History: Oaxaca's Night of the Radishes began in 1897 when farm
 ers carved oversized radishes to entice holiday shoppers\, becoming an off
 icial civic festival.\nTraditions: Artisans sculpt Nativity scenes\, dance
 rs\, and mythical beasts from radishes\, competing for prizes as bands and
  crowds stroll the zocalo.\nFeasting: Cena Tradicional Oaxaquena de Invier
 no features an ensalada de rabanos with lime and cilantro\, tamales de rab
 ano with bright raw radish salsa and atole verde de epazote\, champurrado 
 espeso de cacao y masa\, optional tamales dulces de pina y pasas\, and sip
 s of mezcal artesanal served with orange slices and sal de gusano.\nDay: T
 uesday\n\nIcon: 🥕\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/night-of-the-radishes-traditional-oaxacan-festival.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20251224T070000Z
DTEND:20251225T070000Z
SUMMARY:Christmas Eve
DESCRIPTION:History: Christian communities have kept the Nativity vigil sin
 ce the fourth century\, preparing through liturgies of lessons\, carols\,
  and midnight Mass.\nTraditions: Families light Advent candles\, attend se
 rvices\, exchange modest gifts\, and observe the tradition of refraining f
 rom meat until the feast begins. Our family brings out the Tió made from
  the tree felled on St. Martin’s Eve\; children sing and tap the log to
  discover sweets and small gifts beneath its blanket.\nFeasting: Italian S
 even Fishes dinners\, Polish wigilia with beet soup and pierogi\, and hot
  cocoa with cookies set the stage for the midnight celebration. Share Torr
 ó d’Agramunt—Catalan honey nougat with toasted hazelnuts or almonds a
 nd edible wafer—during the Christmas Eve Tió festivities.\nDay: Wednesd
 ay\n\nIcon: 🌟\nCategory: religious
CATEGORIES:religious
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/caga-tio.svg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20251225T070000Z
DTEND:20251226T070000Z
SUMMARY:Christmas Day
DESCRIPTION:History: By late antiquity the Western church fixed December 25
  as the Nativity feast\, intertwining with Sol Invictus imagery and evolvi
 ng into a global holiday.\nTraditions: People gather around decorated tree
 s\, sing carols\, exchange gifts\, and share charity with neighbours in th
 e spirit of goodwill.\nFeasting: Roast goose or turkey\, glazed ham\, pota
 toes\, Brussels sprouts\, Christmas pudding\, and yule logs anchor generou
 s holiday tables.\nDay: Thursday\n\nIcon: 🎄\nCategory: celebration
CATEGORIES:celebration
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/image33.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20251226T070000Z
DTEND:20251227T070000Z
SUMMARY:St. Stephen's Day (Boxing Day)
DESCRIPTION:History: St. Stephen\, the first Christian martyr\, is honoured
  on 26 December\; in Britain and Ireland the date also became Boxing Day f
 or giving to servants and the poor.\nTraditions: Foxhunts\, charity drives
 \, and visits to friends are common\, while in Ireland the Wren Boys once 
 paraded door to door collecting donations.\nFeasting: Leftover Christmas r
 oasts become hearty pies\, paired with bubble-and-squeak\, mince pies\, an
 d mugs of spiced ale.\nDay: Friday\n\nIcon: ⛪\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/stefans-feast-stone.png
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20251104T055848Z
DTSTART:20251227T070000Z
DTEND:20251228T070000Z
SUMMARY:Feast of the Holy Innocents (Childermas Banquet)
DESCRIPTION:History: Childermas commemorates the Bethlehem children slain u
 nder King Herod\; medieval English households kept 28 December as the Feas
 t of the Holy Innocents\, centring prayers and hospitality on the youngest
  family members.\nTraditions: Households set a whimsical winter table with
  white candles\, holly berries\, and silver bells\, portion dishes in chil
 d-sized servings\, and invite the youngest at table to announce each cours
 e as the day's presider.\nFeasting: A Childermas banquet pours Baby Bellin
 i aperitifs of sparkling wine or apple cider with white peach puree\, then
  cradles baby beet bisque swirled with creme fraiche and dill over star-cu
 t rye croutons\; baby greens toss with baby tomatoes\, carrots\, and corn 
 in honey-citrus vinaigrette with toasted almonds and edible flowers\; slow
 -smoked baby back ribs lacquered in pomegranate glaze and herb-roasted Cor
 nish hens with clementine pan jus arrive beside duck-fat roasted baby pota
 toes\, baby corn gratin with nutmeg-Parmesan crust\, and braised baby onio
 ns in balsamic reduction\; serve pillowy milk rolls with whipped honey but
 ter\, finish with baby cheesecakes topped in spiced pear-cranberry compote
  or tableside baby bananas Foster\, and toast with Babycham or warm milk p
 unch dusted with nutmeg.\nDay: Saturday\n\nIcon: 👶\nCategory: cultural
CATEGORIES:cultural
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
X-IMAGE:/images/baby-back-ribs.jpg
END:VEVENT
